{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01URs3xrRy6ThhnFLFfVZ5G8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be done.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment using Unix utilities as a test bed. It generates Go implementations from formal specifications and verifies them via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/<name>/` - One directory per utility with main.go and tests\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing framework\n- `pkg/sys/` - System call abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n\n\n- `pkg/hashutil/` - Hash/checksum utilities\n- `pkg/sizeparse/` - Size parsing\n- `pkg/encutil/` - Encoding utilities\n- `docs/specs/` - SRDs, use cases, test suites\n- `magefiles/` - Build automation\n\n**Current State**: Looking at the requirement states, I can see which SRDs have requirements ready for implementation. From the code status, I'm checking what's been implemented across the releases—version 00.0 is partial with format and testutils not yet started, while version 01.1 hasn't begun work on cat.\n\nScanning through the remaining releases, most of the utilities are fully implemented: sponge, du, true, false, yes, basename, dirname, tee, head, seq, and wc are all done. Some releases have partial progress—version 03.1 is missing nproc but has the system info commands, version 04.1 is missing logname but has the path utilities, and versions 05.0 through 05.3 have scattered gaps like echo, tac, cut, and expand that still need work. Version 04.0 hasn't started on ls yet.\n\nLooking ahead, the checksum utilities in 05.4 are partially done with md5sum and sha256sum implemented but sha1sum and sha512sum still pending. Version 06.0 has most file operations except mktemp, and 06.1 is missing groups but has the environment and user info commands. Version 07.0 has tr and tail but sort is still needed, while 07.1 hasn't touched cp, mv, and rm at all. Everything from version 08.0 onward remains completely untouched.\n\nChecking the requirement states, I can see which SRDs have their requirements marked as ready—srd056-cp, srd057-mv, and srd058-rm all have R1.1-R4.4 ready for release 07.1, while srd060-date has all requirements ready for 08.0, and srd061-sleep is also listed. The srd004-ts has all requirements ready but that's for release 15.0, which is much further out. Continuing through the release checklist with more utilities across versions 09.0 through 10.1, covering checksum tools, encoding utilities, and file permission commands. Looking at the next batch of utilities from srd103 through srd114, I can see they're all marked as ready across releases 12.1 through 15.0. Now I need to identify which requirements are missing from this list. I need to focus on what's actually in requirement_states rather than trying to infer missing entries. If an SRD isn't listed there, I should assume its requirements are already complete or skipped, so I shouldn't propose new tasks for it. The constraint is clear: only propose tasks for R-items with \"ready\" status, and ignore anything that's not explicitly marked as ready in the system.\n\nLooking at which releases have ready requirements, the earliest ones appear to be Release 07.1 with several SRDs marked as \"none\" for code status, followed by Release 08.0 with items that haven't started yet. But I need to be careful about earlier releases like 00.0 and 01.1 that show \"not started\" code but have no requirement_states entries—those shouldn't be proposed since they're not in the ready list. I can only propose tasks for R-items marked as \"ready\" in requirement_states, so the earliest release I can work with is 07.1 (cp, mv, rm). Looking at the git history for srd056-cp, I see that R2.1-R2.4 were just completed in a recent task, so only R3.1-R4.4 are