e way pkg/ packages do. The contract requirement only applies to pkg/ packages with exported APIs, not cmd/ utilities like mv and rm. Since cmd/cp was created directly without a contract task in its first stitch task, I can follow the same pattern here. I need to set up the SRD file paths using the naming convention for srd057-mv.yaml and srd058-rm.yaml, then construct the four tasks.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the dependencies between these tasks: cp R3.1-R3.4 builds on existing code, cp R4.1-R4.4 depends on the previous cp task, mv R1.1-R1.4 is independent, and mv R2.1-R2.4 depends on its first range.\n\nFollowing the canonical ordering—types before consumers, implementation before tests, then alphabetically by file path—I'm arranging them as: cp R3.1-R3.4, cp R4.1-R4.4, mv R1.1-R1.4, then mv R2.1-R2.4.\n\nNow I need to write out the descriptions for each task.  Based on the release notes and the SRD naming conventions, I can infer what each requirement set likely covers: cp's R3 and R4 probably handle recursive operations and symlink handling, while mv's R1 and R2 cover basic moves and cross-filesystem operations.\n\nNow I'm structuring the task descriptions with the key sections each one needs—the SRD file as required reading, the specific Go files to create or modify, the exact R-items to implement, relevant architectural patterns to follow, and the standard build/test/vet acceptance criteria. For cp, since the first two requirement sets are already complete, I'll mark those files as modifications rather than new creations.\n\nThe task title format should follow the pattern I see in the logs: `cmd/<utility>: srd<NNN> R<X.Y>-R<X.Z>`, keeping it straightforward without the orchestrator prefixes. Since I don't have the actual SRD content in front of me, I'll keep the requirement descriptions generic and let the stitch agent reference the SRD file directly for the implementation details.\n\nBut I'm noticing a tension in the planning constitution—P2 says to always split