nageable within their scope.\n\nLet me verify the status of these requirements. Looking at the SRD, cp has R3.1-R3.4 and R4.1-R4.4 marked as ready, meaning R1.1-R2.4 are already complete. For mv, R1.1-R1.4 and R2.1-R2.4 are all ready, so I'm working with the full set of basic requirements.\n\nNow checking the mv R3 group more carefully—it looks like there are only three items (R3.1, R3.2, R3.3) rather than four, which means the total count is 15 items across all groups for mv.\n\nGiven that I can only propose four tasks and cp still has two complete groups remaining, I should prioritize finishing cp first since it's already partially done, then move into mv's requirements. So my plan is to tackle cp's R3 and R4 groups next, followed by starting mv with its R1 and R2 groups, which leaves mv's R3 and R4 groups plus all of rm for subsequent batches.\n\nNow I'm structuring the task output with the first item being cp's R3.1-R3.4 requirements, which involves modifying the existing cp implementation and adding differential test cases to cover those specific requirements. I'm realizing I need to be more specific about what R3 and R4 actually entail for the cp command—the requirements should map to concrete functionality like basic file copying, rather than staying generic references to the SRD. I'm settling on a cleaner approach where each requirement references the SRD directly—the stitch agent will read the SRD to understand what needs implementing, and the required_reading section ensures it has access to both the specification and any existing code. For the cp task, the agent will see what's already been completed in main.go, and for mv, it'll be starting fresh with R1.1-R1.4. Writing YAML output...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":51406,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":51406},"output_tokens":27,